WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.044 --> 00:00:08.360
It only takes a few seconds for one of the most beautiful and peaceful experiences nature has to offer to turn into a disaster.

2
00:00:08.361 --> 00:00:19.281
In November of 1998, four experienced young Canadians were cross-country skiing through the spectacular Rocky Mountains in Kokanee Glacier Park, British Columbia.

3
00:00:19.282 --> 00:00:28.290
The next moment, the group found itself plummeting down a steep slope, tossed about by one of the most powerful forces in nature: an avalanche.

4
00:00:28.291 --> 00:00:32.891
Three members of the group survived the dangerous wall of flowing ice and snow.

5
00:00:32.892 --> 00:00:42.817
But one, Michel Trudeau, the 23-year-old son of Pierre Trudeau, a former prime minister of Canada, was swept into the icy depths of Lake Kokanee.

6
00:00:42.818 --> 00:00:46.715
Unfortunately, no one could reach him in time and he drowned.

7
00:00:46.716 --> 00:00:55.135
Trudeau's killer was an enormous mass of snow mingled with ice, soil, pebbles, and possibly boulders that rushed down the mountainside.

8
00:00:55.136 --> 00:01:04.749
Known in some regions as a "snowslide," an avalanche is almost always a terrible, destructive event, one that few plants and animals have the ability to survive.

9
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:10.517
Fortunately, avalanches are not everyday occurrences in most mountain regions.

10
00:01:10.518 --> 00:01:23.292
They require four specific conditions before the snow can start sliding: a steep slope, deep snow cover consisting of several distinct layers including at least one weak layer, and a trigger.

11
00:01:23.293 --> 00:01:30.111
They are, therefore, most common in snowy, mountainous regions, such as the Alps or the Himalayas.

12
00:01:30.112 --> 00:01:40.881
An avalanche occurs when the force of gravity, which is always trying to pull snow downhill, exceeds the force between the molecules of ice that keep snowflakes stuck together.

13
00:01:40.882 --> 00:01:45.830
The layers of snow that build up over time, however, aren't all the same.

14
00:01:45.831 --> 00:01:54.275
For example, layers of snow laid down during extremely cold temperatures will be different from those deposited during warm spells.

15
00:01:54.276 --> 00:02:01.972
The layers will have different moisture levels, and the amount of moisture in the snow affects how well snowflakes bind together.

16
00:02:01.973 --> 00:02:11.895
If a lower layer is composed of ice crystals that don't stick together well, the layers of snow on top can suddenly break free and begin coasting downhill.

17
00:02:11.896 --> 00:02:15.580
A common trigger is often nothing more than a loud noise.

18
00:02:15.581 --> 00:02:22.575
When the conditions are just right, thunder or even a loud shout can be enough to set the deposit of snow in motion.

19
00:02:22.576 --> 00:02:31.353
For this reason, mountain guides often demand that hikers keep absolutely silent when crossing areas where avalanches are common.

20
00:02:31.354 --> 00:02:37.401
Ski resort operators, however, use the ability of noise to set off an avalanche to their advantage.

21
00:02:37.402 --> 00:02:44.557
To avoid dangerous accumulations of snow, they trigger small avalanches with explosives, which create shock waves.

22
00:02:44.558 --> 00:02:53.074
These shock waves shake the weak layers of a snowpack enough to break the bonds between the layers of snow and initiate a snowslide.

23
00:02:53.075 --> 00:02:58.561
This way, there is a lower chance that a hiker or skier could trigger one and die as a result.

24
00:02:58.562 --> 00:03:03.198
Predicting the timing of a natural avalanche is almost impossible.

25
00:03:03.199 --> 00:03:08.985
But experienced mountain guides and snow experts can often tell where one is likely to happen.

26
00:03:08.986 --> 00:03:15.719
Nearly all avalanches, as much as 90 percent, occur on mountain slopes of 30 to 45 degrees.

27
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:26.569
Almost none happen on slopes of less than 25 degreesi—t's too flat—or on inclines steeper than 50 degrees, where snow never gets a chance to build up.

28
00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:34.456
Avalanches most commonly begin above the tree line, where there are no trees to help hold the snowpack in place. Once

29
00:03:34.457 --> 00:03:43.951
an avalanche gets started, however, it can continue down a mountainside well below the tree line, through narrow gullies and along cleared roads. Like

30
00:03:43.952 --> 00:03:50.177
anything that falls toward the ground, the farther an avalanche travels, the more speed it picks up. And

31
00:03:50.178 --> 00:03:53.872
the faster it travels, the more force it acquires. The

32
00:03:53.873 --> 00:04:03.753
largest avalanches move so fast that they can produce a furious wind while the snow tumbles down a mountain, steamrolling almost everything in their paths. Even

33
00:04:03.754 --> 00:04:09.105
thick stands of trees are no match for the power of a sizable avalanche. Entire

34
00:04:09.106 --> 00:04:14.920
villages have found themselves buried under several meters of snow in a matter of minutes. Happily,

35
00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:22.754
few avalanches take place in areas that are properly maintained, like ski resorts and popular hiking trails. Nevertheless,

36
00:04:22.755 --> 00:04:31.730
people are buried alive every year because they were in the wrong place at the wrong time, and did not take precautions. According

37
00:04:31.731 --> 00:04:39.892
to the Colorado Avalanche Information Center, the way to avoid getting caught in an avalanche is to avoid dangerous slopes. If

38
00:04:39.893 --> 00:04:47.355
venturing into remote mountains in the winter, you should carry an inexpensive, handheld tool called an inclinometer. Smaller

39
00:04:47.356 --> 00:04:52.695
than a paperback book, an inclinometer measures the angle of a mountainside. This

40
00:04:52.696 --> 00:04:56.378
simple piece of equipment could save your life. The

41
00:04:56.379 --> 00:05:06.821
Colorado Center, which has a vast amount of experience with avalanches, also recommends you keep an eye out for patterns in the snow that show how it has been laid down. Knowing

42
00:05:06.822 --> 00:05:13.811
how to identify snow-covered areas that pose an avalanche risk, however, takes years of experience. A

43
00:05:13.812 --> 00:05:20.187
warning sign that is easier to see is bent trees that show where previous avalanches have taken place. Also,

44
00:05:20.188 --> 00:05:25.182
snow that makes a cracking or hollow sound could be unstable. You

45
00:05:25.183 --> 00:05:31.498
should also watch for rocky outcrops or large trees that could prevent runaway slides. Staying

46
00:05:31.499 --> 00:05:36.188
close to these will show you safer routes around dangerous areas. If,

47
00:05:36.189 --> 00:05:45.556
despite these efforts, you are still caught in an avalanche, try first to get to the side of the snowslide, where the force of the moving snow is weakest. Grab

48
00:05:45.557 --> 00:05:50.476
onto any heavy object within reach, like a tree or large rock. If

49
00:05:50.477 --> 00:05:56.573
that's not possible, "swim" with the avalanche and try to stay on top of the snow. Although

50
00:05:56.574 --> 00:06:07.497
there are many tragic stories like Michel Trudeau's, there are also plenty of examples of people who manage to survive being buried beneath more than a meter of snow. Rescuers

51
00:06:07.498 --> 00:06:14.692
found one young woman buried by an avalanche in the mountains of Utah for almost two hours. Although

52
00:06:14.693 --> 00:06:29.082
she couldn't remember too many details, experts suspect that, before the avalanche came to a rest, she moved her head and shoulders as much as possible, creating a small pocket beneath the snow with enough air to keep her alive. The

53
00:06:29.083 --> 00:06:35.926
word "avalanche" comes from a word in a French dialect, avalance , which means simply "descent."

54
00:06:35.927 --> 00:06:40.021
But the image behind this word is one of a great unstoppable force.

55
00:06:40.022 --> 00:06:48.862
It has inspired the name of a professional hockey team, the Colorado Avalanche, and even a sport-utility vehicle, the Chevrolet Avalanche.

56
00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:59.406
While the image of tons of rolling snow thundering down a mountainside might be a source of awe and wonder, an avalanche remains one of the most dangerous forces that nature can unleash.

